## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a complete edge-CV pipeline: real-time monocular 3D pose with per-object velocity, occlusion robustness, and rapid on-device adaptation to unseen parts. Emphasizes practical data strategies and evaluation in an industrial setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 3D pose estimation
- Per-instance velocity prediction for planning
- On-device few-shot adaptation (5-shot)
- Occlusion handling and data fusion strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass with adaptation trigger
function forward(frame, exemplars){
  let features = backbone(frame);
  let detections = detector(features);
  let poses = poseHead(features, detections);
  let vel = velocityHead(features, detections, previous);
  if (exemplars) adapt(poses, exemplars);
  return {detections, poses, vel};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify robustness to occlusion in your evaluation?
- What ablation would you run to isolate the velocity head contribution?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates real-time monocular computer vision for avatar-driven video conferencing, with emphasis on three critical constraints: sub-30ms latency for natural interaction, on-device privacy for user trust, and rapid on-device adaptation for personalized gesture vocabularies. Success requires co-designing efficient models, privacy-preserving pipelines, and lightweight adaptation mechanisms—skills essential for building consumer-grade AR/VR experiences at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular landmark detection, gesture recognition, and avatar rig mapping
- Edge latency budgets and resource-aware model architectures
- Few-shot on-device adaptation using synthetic and real data
- Privacy-by-design constraints and rigorous evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton of the avatar estimator outline
class AvatarEstimator {
  constructor(model) {
    this.model = model;
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  infer(frame) {
    const landmarks = this.model.detect(frame);
    const avatarParams = this.mapToAvatar(landmarks);
    return avatarParams;
  }

  mapToAvatar(landmarks) {
    // Map 2D landmarks to 3D avatar rig parameters
    return {
      blendShapes: this.computeBlendShapes(landmarks),
      handPose: this.estimateHandPose(landmarks)
    };
  }
}
```
